612 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (The gut-brain axis is a bidirectional communication pathway between the gut and the brain, linking emotional and cognitive centers of the brain with peripheral intestinal functions. This connection explains why gut health can influence mood, stress response, and even cognitive function, and why stress can have such a significant impact on digestion. How Communication Happens: Communication occurs through nerves, hormones, and biochemical signals. The Vagus Nerve: This is the primary nerve connecting the gut to the brain, responsible for carrying signals from the gut to the brain and vice versa. Hormonal Pathways: Hormones and neurotransmitters like cortisol \(stress hormone\) and serotonin \(mood stabilizer\) play crucial roles in this connection. Immune System Signaling: Inflammation or immune responses in the gut can send signals to the brain, influencing mood and behavior.)
/E (The gut-brain axis is a bidirectional communication pathway between the gut and the brain, linking emotional and cognitive centers of the brain with peripheral intestinal functions. This connection explains why gut health can influence mood, stress response, and even cognitive function, and why stress can have such a significant impact on digestion. How Communication Happens: Communication occurs through nerves, hormones, and biochemical signals. The Vagus Nerve: This is the primary nerve connecting the gut to the brain, responsible for carrying signals from the gut to the brain and vice versa. Hormonal Pathways: Hormones and neurotransmitters like cortisol \(stress hormone\) and serotonin \(mood stabilizer\) play crucial roles in this connection. Immune System Signaling: Inflammation or immune responses in the gut can send signals to the brain, influencing mood and behavior.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
